.TH AIO_WRITE 3 "July 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
aio_write, aio_fsync \- asynchronous write and file synchronization
.SH LIBRARY
Realtime library (librt, \-lrt)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <aio.h>
.PP
.BI "int aio_write(struct aiocb *" aiocbp );
.br
.BI "int aio_fsync(int " op ", struct aiocb *" aiocbp );
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR aio_write ()
queues an asynchronous write request described by
.RB * aiocbp
and returns immediately.  It writes
.I aio_nbytes
bytes from the buffer
.I aio_buf
to the file descriptor
.I aio_fildes
starting at offset
.IR aio_offset .
See
.BR aio_read (3)
for the layout of
.BR "struct aiocb" .
.PP
.BR aio_fsync ()
queues an asynchronous
.BR fsync (2)
of
.IR aio_fildes .
.I op
must be
.B O_SYNC
or
.BR O_DSYNC .
The remaining
.B aiocb
fields other than
.I aio_fildes
and
.I aio_sigevent
are ignored.
.PP
Both operations deliver completion notification as described by
.IR aio_sigevent ,
and their status is retrieved with
.BR aio_error (3)
and
.BR aio_return (3).
The underlying I/O runs on a librt worker-thread pool, so the calling thread
is never blocked.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success 0 is returned (the request was queued).  On error \-1 is returned
and
.I errno
is set.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EAGAIN
Insufficient resources to queue the request, or the number of outstanding
asynchronous I/O operations would exceed
.BR {AIO_MAX} ,
the limit reported by
.BR sysconf (_SC_AIO_MAX).
An operation is outstanding from submission until its status is retrieved
with
.BR aio_return (3).
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I aiocbp
is NULL;
.I aio_reqprio
or
.I aio_offset
is negative; or
.RB ( aio_fsync )
.I op
is neither
.B O_SYNC
nor
.BR O_DSYNC .
When prioritized I/O is supported
.RB ( sysconf (_SC_PRIORITIZED_IO)),
a lower
.I aio_reqprio
is a higher priority and its request is serviced first.
.PP
Errors from the underlying
.BR pwrite (2)
or
.BR fsync (2)
are reported asynchronously and retrieved with
.BR aio_error (3).
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
struct aiocb cb = { 0 };
cb.aio_fildes = fd;
cb.aio_buf    = "data";
cb.aio_nbytes = 4;
cb.aio_sigevent.sigev_notify = SIGEV_NONE;
aio_write(&cb);
const struct aiocb *l[1] = { &cb };
aio_suspend(l, 1, NULL);
if (aio_error(&cb) == 0)
        printf("wrote %zd bytes\\n", aio_return(&cb));
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aio_read (3),
.BR aio_suspend (3),
.BR aio_error (3),
.BR aio_return (3)
